WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.128
For discrete variables, Bayesian networks often use conditional probability tables, or CPTs.

00:00:04.128 --> 00:00:08.827
A discrete variable has a set of named states, such as true or false,

00:00:08.827 --> 00:00:10.840
high or low, working or failed.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.204
A root node has no parents, so it uses a prior probability distribution.

00:00:15.204 --> 00:00:18.224
For example, Weather might have probabilities for sunny, cloudy,

00:00:18.224 --> 00:00:20.574
and rainy before considering any parent variable.

00:00:20.574 --> 00:00:23.595
A child node has probabilities conditioned on its parents.

00:00:23.595 --> 00:00:27.958
Suppose a simple network has Weather and Sprinkler as parents of Wet Ground.

00:00:27.958 --> 00:00:31.986
The probability of Wet Ground depends on whether the weather is rainy

00:00:31.986 --> 00:00:33.999
and whether the sprinkler is on.

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:39.034
The CPT for Wet Ground has a row for each relevant combination of parent states.

00:00:39.034 --> 00:00:41.719
Each row should form a valid probability distribution.

00:00:41.719 --> 00:00:43.062
In a true-or-false row,

00:00:43.062 --> 00:00:47.425
the probability of true plus the probability of false should sum to one.

00:00:47.425 --> 00:00:51.453
The graph lets the joint probability distribution be factored into local terms.

00:00:51.453 --> 00:00:54.138
One compact way to write the idea is:

00:00:54.138 --> 00:00:59.508
the probability of all variables together equals the product of each variable's probability given its parents.

00:00:59.508 --> 00:01:04.207
In words, instead of building one enormous table that lists every possible state combination,

00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:07.563
the Bayesian network breaks the model into smaller local relationships.

00:01:07.563 --> 00:01:11.255
That is one reason Bayesian networks are useful for practical modeling.

00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:12.933
Factorization is not magic compression.

00:01:12.933 --> 00:01:16.290
It depends on the conditional independence assumptions in the graph.

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.989
If the graph leaves out an important dependency, the factorized model can be misleading.

00:01:20.989 --> 00:01:23.674
Also, not every Bayesian network uses simple CPTs.

00:01:23.674 --> 00:01:26.359
Continuous or hybrid networks may use Gaussian distributions,

00:01:26.359 --> 00:01:29.044
regression-style conditional models, or other local probability distributions.

00:01:29.044 --> 00:01:33.072
The core idea remains the same: local distributions, organized by the graph,

00:01:33.072 --> 00:01:34.750
define the larger probabilistic model.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.770
In practice, CPT design is often a modeling bottleneck.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.127
Too many parents can create a table with many rows,

00:01:41.127 --> 00:01:44.148
and each row needs probability values that mean something.

00:01:44.148 --> 00:01:46.161
Modelers sometimes simplify states, combine variables,

00:01:46.161 --> 00:01:49.518
or use structured conditional distributions to keep the model manageable.

00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:52.874
The goal is not to make the largest possible table.

00:01:52.874 --> 00:01:56.902
The goal is to represent the relationships at the level of detail

00:01:56.902 --> 00:01:58.580
needed for the reasoning question.
